[Music]  this morning on the dish a chef with  globally inspired food and an  increasingly international reach born in  New Jersey Ken Oringer loved great  restaurants and after college decided to  make cooking his career he worked for  star chefs such as David Burke and John  George and had jobs in New York in San  Francisco before starting his own global  brand working with fellow chef and  business partner Jaime Bisson  Bissonnette their JK group runs Boston's  kapa and little donkey and their  best-known venture Toro a tapas bar that  now has locations in Boston New York  Bangkok and Dubai chef Ken Oringer good  morning welcome to the dish thanks for  being here morning guys so psyched to be  here this is quite a table and some  incredible presentation tell us what's  in this show well let's start with this  let's start with this is what we call  the one in the grapefruit which to have  people walking around a restaurant  carrying grapefruits to me is fun and  all our restaurants we just want people  to have fun and that's in the grapefruit  is funny obviously great food so this is  kind of a a play in a margarita but not  as sweet a little more interesting and  just something that sets the tone for  little donkey which is global small  plates which when chefs cook sometimes  they get bored with cooking like Italian  food or Spanish food or a certain type  of food so when people come over to my  apartment or Jamie my business partners  apartment we kind of just take whatever  we have and we wing a meal and we just  have fun cooking spontaneous really  eclectic food the things that you love  what do we have net fried chicken  sandwich that's what this is so this is  a Thai style fried chicken sandwich  where the chicken is marinated in pickle  brine there with the green papaya slaw  basically on a Martin's potato roll and  then we also have sweet potatoes with a  little bit of Molay and savory granola  we have cucumber salad with buttermilk  and tomatillo salsa verde and for a  holiday time I love serving clams casino  but being in New England we have the  best razor clams I think anywhere in the  world so we do  or clams Casino and then for dessert we  have profiteroles filled with matcha  which is green tea ice cream and then we  have pokey sticks which are these alike  so they shop things from Japan which I  think everybody's probably eaten pokey  sticks hopefully in their life and we  just basically crumble these on top and  then just kind of serve it where it's  kind of a Japanese Street foodie little  desserts now where does this come from  for you ever since I was a little kid my  parents encouraged me to cook and to  read cookbooks and it's something you  weren't reading always whatever you were  reading cookbooks at what age six years  old and watching Julia Child  Julia Child and reading Gourmet magazine  which my I couldn't wait for it to come  every time it came in the mail and and I  just was on his path I was obsessed with  food and I've kind of never given up  that obsession and I just love it still  so how does a Jersey boy master sushi  and Mexican food and Italian food you've  also have a Spanish tapas restaurant  it's a lot of traveling all my  restaurants have been pretty much the  product of my travels I think traveling  is the most important thing that any  young chef can do whether it be two  ethnic neighborhoods in your city or  your town or whether it be going  overseas or anywhere just exposing your  palate to as many different things as  you can creates taste memories which I  think are so important so when you start  thinking about food instead of thinking  about well I can use a little acid on  this chicken sandwich I'm just gonna put  lemon on it if you think about what kind  of acid they use in India for instance  you say well I'm gonna put am tor powder  which is a green mango powder that they  use in India for acidic touches you  could say well I'm gonna make a slaw but  I'm going to put my powder instead of  lemon juice and I'll give it a whole  nother dimension and we're always trying  to create food that has very unique  dimension you had a very successful solo  career and then you partnered with Jamie  Bissonnette and and you've had an  incredibly successful partnership what  why does it work and why did you just  decide to partner up it's I mean you  guys go way back right yeah we go way  back it's that you can only do so much  by yourself I think between the two of  us we have so much energy and we have  like a yin and yang you know he's this  guy with tattoos from head to toe  you know he's younger than me he's  almost like almost not quite like a son  but I mean definitely younger and and so  we have so many different approaches to  how we run our restaurants and how we  look at food and it's nice because we've  been working together for so long that  you can bring out so many more things  from each other because we're both very  critical and and we're not afraid to  take criticism which i think is the key  what is the biggest challenge though of  not being able to make it just what you  want you have to incorporate what  somebody else wants but sometimes you  can maneuver a little bit for something  that if it's like in a marriage if  there's something you really feel  strongly about you have to respect what  what your partner says about it you have  to say you know what I'll let you in  this battle but you know let's move on  to the next and and not make a big deal  about it well let's have you sign the  dish and I know your parents are here  with you so maybe they are the answer  I'm not certain but if you could have  this meal with anyone past or present  who would it be I mean I'm all about  family I've two little kids Luca and ver  van and and my wife Celine and to me  eating food with family there's nothing  better and that's why I first got into  cooking and and to me if I can have a  ton of food at a table the more people  the merrier and just family and and  friends and a big wooden fire cooking  and and I'm in heaven and plenty of  mezcal or injure thank you so much and  for more on can in the dish head to our  website at CBS this morning dot-com

